    "created_at": "2026-11-13T14:54:47Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-11-13T14:54:47Z",
    "body": "Thanks for the detailed reproduction. Here is what PR #1419 changes.\n\n- Your laid-flat job now generates. When a slice plane meets the mesh 3+ ways, the slicer retries the model as the union of its closed solids: each solid is sliced on its own and the sections are united. With the sample laid flat, 3D Pocket and 3D Finish both come back `ok`. The program's stock sim finds no gouge, and the finish envelope's gouge reading stays under tolerance.\n- The retry only runs when every body is provably a closed, outward-wound solid. Solids welded together along a shared edge or face, an open body, or an inward cavity shell are still refused.\n- That refusal now names the model instead of \"invalid machining parameters\": \"the source model cannot be sliced: the model's surfaces meet 3 or more ways at z = ... (non-manifold contact: ...) - repair the mesh, or keep each solid a separate closed shell\".\n- One thing you may still see: on this model, 3D Finish S1's post is held by a finish-envelope *rest* finding (about 4.6 mm of material left on the steep walls a parallel finish does not reach). It shows up the same way on the body pairs that sliced fine before, so it is how 3D Finish behaves on this geometry, not part of this bug.\n- Models that sliced before take exactly the same path as before.\n",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
